  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 PM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nSimilar to yesterday, the Day Cloud Phase RGB indicates some high-\nlevel cloud cover moving in over the region this afternoon. \nTemperatures have warmed into the mid to upper 30s under the mostly \nclear skies this morning.\n\nTonight marks the end of our period of anomalously low minimum \ntemperatures, with low teens in the interior west and low twenties \neast and along the lakeshore. Cloud cover will continue to move in \nfrom the south ahead of the approaching Atlantic Low progressing up \nthe East Coast. Temperatures will warm to 0-10 degrees above normal \nheading into the weekend as highs reach the upper 30s to low 40s and \nlows reach the low-20s inland (low 30s along the lakeshore). \nPrecipitation chances associated with the Atlantic Low have tracked \nfarther east than previously forecast, keeping Thanksgiving Day and \nFriday daytime dry throughout the U.P.\n\nPrecipitation chances increase to 20-30% late Friday evening for the \nwestern portion of the forecast area as a shallow 500mb shortwave \ntrough associated with a vertically stacked Alberta Clipper over \nManitoba moves over the area. Impactful precipitation amounts are \nnot expected (15% chance for greater than 0.10\") for Friday night \nthrough Saturday. Current model guidance indicate that the primary \nmode of precipitation will be light snow.\n\nChances for precipitation across the U.P. increase further Saturday \nnight into Sunday as the passage of the Manitoba low to the north of \nthe Great Lakes increases midlevel moisture and decreases 850mb \ntemperatures. As the Clipper exits the region Sunday evening, the \nchances for widespread snowfall over the western U.P. and rainfall \nover the eastern U.P. diminish, however, the northwesterly winds, \nincreased moisture aloft, and 850mb temperatures falling to around -\n10 degrees Celsius over the lake indicate a favorable set-up for \nlingering lake effect snow Sunday evening into Monday. Additional \nprecipitation chances enter the forecast as another weak Clipper \nsystem moves through the area midweek.",
